00:00:03.679 --> 00:00:05.030 align:start position:0%
 
it's always nice to see your friends  again even if your time together doesn't  always live up to the highest highs  you've experienced in the past  persona 5 strikers is a lot like that it  comes with some special moments that  foster a deeper appreciation for what  persona 5  is and what it represents from the wild  action rpg combat to the summer road  trip premise  characters we know and love get to show  off how much they've grown and prove  they can still kick ass in style  strikers does fall into a few clumsy  tropes along the way and sometimes it  tries a little too hard to recapture  persona 5's magic but when it's all said  and done  i'm glad this reunion happened to begin  with  yeah summer vacation here we come  it's summer vacation after the events of  the original game but before they can  even make plans they're caught in  another round of fighting evil in the  metaverse  and changing hearts this time it's all  across japan  strikers follows a similar flow  established in persona 5 but the context  is quite different both narratively and  gameplay-wise  your first few targets have been  manipulating people's desires in order  to feed their own ambitions  but there's a bigger mystery as to how  and why there's strange behavior in mass  you begin to unveil that tragic paths  have led them down a dark path of  exploiting the metaverse  the broader message isn't to excuse  their behavior or to say that trauma  will surely corrupt its victims  rather that our circumstances and the  people around us or  lack thereof significantly influences  how we internalize and process  pain through each story beat the main  cast relates to these tragic situations  because of what they went through in the  original game  is she beyond helping at this point  i don't know but there has to be someone  who at least  tries we can't just abandon alice  oh lady on it shows just how much our  fellow phantom thieves have grown since  the events of persona  5 and strikers gives them the  opportunity to impart their wisdom  onto others however the story doesn't  always hit these topics with particular  nuance  leaving some of its messaging feeling a  bit shallow dialogue sometimes relies on  surface level exposition or talking in  circles to belabor a point  many of the villains are avatars for a  type of character flaw in a very  matter-of-fact way  and i felt somewhat detached to the  stakes at times although it's not  deliberately explored in depth  persona 5 strikers does have a  surprisingly poignant message about the  role of technology in our lives  the perks the pitfalls the power it  affords certain people  and how it can be abused and as usual  with persona the story also presents  human connection as a source of strength  and by the end of the journey those  themes come into clearer focus this  extends to the new characters who are  great additions and fit well alongside  the phantom thieves  yo phantom thieves  what is that  i am sophia humanity's companion  sophia is the cutesy artificial  intelligence a siri-like phone companion  who takes human form to fight in the  metaverse  her bubbly attitude and gradual  understanding of human mannerisms gives  a warm vibe  everything should go  and we'll have access zenkichi is a  goofball adult but as an inspector with  japan's public security he's an  authority figure that everyone  rightfully distrusts  sorry about that we just despise the  police's off  but his growth is key to persona 5's  themes like defying flawed institutions  in order to make things right which also  helps him rebuild a relationship with  his daughter  the integration of sophia and zenkichi  only feels natural and that's due in  large part to how strikers builds the  story around them  yeah you really came through gramps i  knew we could count on you gramps son  come on guys at least call me by my name  don't feel bad gramps  now this is summer vacation so with an  rv on hand and makoto with a driver's  license this becomes your home away from  home on this road trip  from the sun-soaked beaches of okinawa  to the bustling city center of dotenbury  it's exciting to share the experience of  touring japan with these characters  even though there isn't much to do in  these cities other than shop for items  or talk to npcs and your companions  taking in the scenery is pretty sweet  it's much lighter on the social  simulation elements though you do have  things called requests where you can  take on side objectives  and spend bond points to increase perks  that you can take into battle  there is a calendar that acts as a  timeline for the story but absent are  the pressures of deadlines hanging over  your head you can also leave and enter  dungeons at any checkpoint to recuperate  without penalty  it serves to tighten the narrative but  also creates a more carefree vibe  after all the battles and tragedies that  the phantom thieves overcame  it's nice to see them enjoy life even as  they continue fighting against the  looming evil  it's not always smooth sailing though at  times strikers falls into cringy tropes  that undermine its ethos  for example one villain's metaverse form  is an overweight caricature  using obesity as shorthand for evil it  doesn't even make sense in context  there's also not one but two bath house  scenes that put the female cast in  uncomfortable situations as a  meaningless gag also we can stop picking  on my boy  so ryuji snap out of it ryuji  we're about to head in i know i know  i was so excited last night i couldn't  sleep what you think it's christmas  morning or something  with most of these characters having  troubled pass i'd rather not see them  being treated unfairly amongst friends  with each stop across japan there's work  to be done and the way strikers  faithfully incorporates persona 5's rpg  mechanics and its lavish style  for frenetic musou style action made me  eager to put in that work  you'll explore dungeons as you would in  persona 5 sneaking up on shadows for  ambushes or confronting them to initiate  battles and you jump straight into  real-time action  each character has their own elemental  affinities persona abilities  and unique attack combos you can swap  between any one of the four in your  party to take direct control and make  the most of their capabilities  while you can stop time by pulling up  spells combat moves so quickly that you  need to be consistently attacking or  dodging as you devise ways to exploit  enemy weaknesses  it's a lot to take in for the first few  hours of the game tutorials are late on  thick but battles got chaotic before i  had a firm grasp of things  but the various mechanics started to  make sense and as i began to form  general combat strategies i was just  itching to start fights it's so  satisfying to tear through mobs of  shadows while juggling the challenge of  tougher enemies that hit harder and have  a ton of hp  you can't necessarily get away with  spamming spells it's best to learn how  to use characters specific elemental  combos that are free of sp  cost combine that with a variety of  personas and joker's roster  earning all-out attack opportunities and  executing showtime attacks  you'll feel unstoppable when it all  comes together  each character has their personality and  charm come through with how they  function in battle too  you can even directly control them  during dungeon exploration which is a  sweet touch that lets your favorite  character be the star of your show now  as gratifying as the big fights are this  is a somewhat inelegant combat system  things can get messy when there's an  overwhelming amount to parse on screen  making it hard to telegraph enemy  attacks  and this can pile on in a moment's  notice for devastating results  also the more story focused fights  suffered from having active dialogue  overlapping with each other while other  audio cues  and sound effects and music were layered  on as well and it got overbearing  when it comes to dungeon designs they  work well enough although they rely on  basic formats and aren't nearly as  imaginative as they are in persona 5.  what is an annoyance however is that the  flow is often interrupted by superfluous  dialogue that overexplains each step of  the dungeon's progress  all things considered combat is a  definite highlight strikers  is a strong adaptation and  representation of persona 5's gameplay  qualities  at its best i couldn't help but feel  that persona 5 was always a great match  for action-based combat  now whether you're in combat or going  about your tasks the striker's  soundtrack is going to steal your heart  music is integral to persona's identity  and yet again  the atlas sound team manages to impress  and build on the tremendous work from  persona 5.  old favorites like last surprise and  rivers in the desert have metal and punk  style arrangements with up-tempo  percussion  and carry a new attitude to perfectly  match the chaotic action  the new battle theme what you wish for  is an instant classic with upbeat acid  jazz rock style  each town you visit comes with new  comforting background songs too  one standout being okinawa which  features islander instrumentals and  borrows a melody from the persona 5  anime credit song called infinity  the soundtrack also touches on symphonic  metal but also dark and heavy electronic  tunes  but no song will sell you on the  soundtrack quite like daredevil  it carries the same vibe as life will  change but with a more aggressive  attitude  backed by groovy disco metal  instrumentation and lynn's incredible  vocals  this soundtrack showcases the range of  atlas sound team  and also their ability to make music  that stirs up the spectrum of emotions  and become inseparable from the  experience now once i reach the final  hours of strikers  i was reminded of persona's ability to  create an unmistakable sense of finality  many parts of this game are quite goofy  or hammy but when things come together  towards the end  and the music kicks in at the right time  my heart was fully in it  strikers has a deep cast of characters  and the way it brings the entire crew  together for these final moments  makes the phantom thieves feel whole  everyone puts in the work and everyone  shines in their own right as they finish  the fight  ending the journey on a high note so for  all of its shortcomings  persona 5 strikers reminds me of why i  love the phantom thieves to begin with  and shows us how much they've grown  since we last saw them  once you get your head around it the  action rpg combat is a challenging  thrill  and no one can pull it off in the same  fashion as these characters  i almost forgot i was playing a muso  style game developed by koei tekmo no  megaforce  i think that's a testament to how sharp  strikers is as a persona game  if you have any love for persona 5 would  say you saved the world one more time  with some old friends  thank you

